{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310862,"nonce":"TlT4X9EBFtOYvqCmhAhi1DuBVsw=","title":"Comprehensive session summary for the 2026-06 llama-chat cold-cache compiler-side arc. Original framing (60% streaming heap corruption / ...","message":"- HEAD: **b013900** (\"compiler: refresh stdlib_function_name_map after stdlib merge\"), committed.\n- Track B (user_function_name_map mirror + cranelift call-site name fallback): attempted, measured, **REVERTED** — no observable change; residuals 280007/220015 unchanged.\n- Working tree (per git status at session open): uncommitted edits in stdlib/runtime/nue/runtime adjacent to QTensor/Tensor + GGUFLoader; not part of the b013900 commit set.\n\n\n1. **Original framing:** \"60% streaming heap corruption / worker_pool race.\" Hypothesised mpsc-recv-loop panic-skip in `worker_pool::parallel_rows` was racing first-decode allocations. **WRONG.** The crash class wasn't heap corruption at all — it was trap-stubbed CallDirects whose 4-byte `udf` corrupted whatever frame ran them.\n\n2. **worker_pool 5-piece fix** (Mutex+Condvar barrier, catch_unwind, startup barrier, env hatch, eager force_init_global). Result: **regression to 0/10**, uniform W0020 trap-stub cascade in `LlamaArch.build` BEFORE `[gen] streaming...` printed. tiny-transformer still passed.\n\n3. **Narrowed bisect — barrier-only** (kept just parking_lot Mutex+Condvar + env hatch, dropped catch_unwind/startup-barrier/force_init). Result: **still 0/10**, plus tiny-transformer ALSO failed. Remaining culprit was either the barrier shape itself or the parking_lot dep introduction.\n\n4. **Full revert + cache scrub** (`find . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +` + `cargo clean`). Discovered: HEAD itself trap-stubs cold-cache — there was no \"stable ~60% baseline\" to revert TO. The 4/10 historic baseline was warm-cache state confounded with cold.\n\n5. **Identified the real root:** stale-CallDirect trap-stub cascade from `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` gaps. Specifically, `stdlib_function_name_map` was seeded during user-package compile with PRE-MERGE local stub IrFunctionIds; the stdlib merge's `id_replacements` renumber updated `mir_module.functions` but NOT the name map's VALUES; the secondary fixup then used those stale stub ids as an oracle and OVERWROTE correct cross-module CallDirects with dead ids.\n\n6. **b013900 landed.** Three pieces:\n   - `stdlib_function_name_map` `id_replacements` refresh after Main.hx stdlib merge (closes the staleness window).\n   - Sweep 1 hardening: don't count `cached_stdlib_mir` ids as \"live\" when deciding whether to drop a map entry.\n   - `qualified_name` on `register_stdlib_mir_forward_ref` stubs so per-CallDirect tertiary fallback can fire.\n   - Path E strict assert: any still-unresolved cross-module CallDirect becomes loud `E0600` / `E0420` compile error rather than silent W0020 trap-stub.\n\n7. **Track B attempt (post-b013900):** Symmetric `user_function_name_map` mirror — BTreeMap on `CompilationUnit`, seed loop after each user module merge, `id_replacements` renumber sweep, parallel walk in `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs`, `qualified_name` population on user functions in `cranelift_backend.rs::declare_function`, call-site last-resort lookup via `qualified_name_to_func`. Measured: **no-change**. Reverted.\n\n8. **Final post-fix measurements** (per next section).\n\n\n| Scenario | Run count | Success | Paris-canonical match | Exit histogram |\n|----------|-----------|---------|-----------------------|----------------|\n| Cold-clean canonical \"What is the capital of France?\" | 10 | 9 | 9 | `{\"0\":9, \"133\":1}` |\n| Cold-clean short prompt | 5 | 4 | n/a | `{\"0\":4, \"133\":1}` |\n| Warm-BLADE canonical | 5 | 5 | 5 | `{\"0\":5}` |\n\nThe single remaining exit 133 (SIGTRAP, Trace/BPT) per cold cycle is content-dependent and lives at the first decode iteration (KVCache vicinity). It is NOT the trap-stub-cascade class b013900 closed.\n\nLib/regression sweep: **709/709** lib tests pass; **127–128/128** haxe regression pass (the 128th is the pre-existing `test_exception` SIGTRAP flake, not a session regression); **tiny-transformer** green.\n\n\n**Does close:**\n- The stdlib_function_name_map staleness window (renumber refresh + Sweep 1 hardening + qualified_name on stubs).\n- The silent-trap-stub failure mode for stdlib CallDirects via Path E: any unresolved cross-module stdlib CallDirect now emits loud E0600 / E0420 at compile time rather than a 4-byte udf at runtime.\n- Cold-clean canonical paris from 0/10 → 9/10. Warm-BLADE from variable → 5/5.\n\n**Does NOT close:**\n- The USER→USER CallDirect renumber gap. Three residual W0020 callees persist in every cold run:\n  - `nue.arch.BertArch.build → IrFunctionId(280007)`\n  - `nue.arch.LlamaArch.build → IrFunctionId(280007)`\n  - `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build → IrFunctionId(220015)`\n  These are user-package symbols (`nue.arch.*`, `nue.loader.*`) — they were never in any map b013900 maintains. The intended scope-extension was Track B; with Track B reverted, these remain stuck on whatever non-deterministic dispatch state happens to be live at JIT time.\n- The runtime SIGTRAP 133 at first decode iteration (separate bug class, see below).\n\n\nAdding a symmetric `user_function_name_map` is INSUFFICIENT on its own. The data is either (a) not being populated for callees 280007 / 220015 specifically, or (b) populated but the seed runs AFTER the fixup pass walks the callers' modules, so the lookup misses despite the data being present moments later, or (c) the IrFunctionIds in question are monomorphization specialisations whose `qualified_name` is `None`, so the qname-keyed map can't address them by name regardless of timing.\n\nTrack B's source patches were sound (defensive correctness — closes a real future-defensible asymmetry between the stdlib and user paths) but did not reach the observed failure mode. The next iteration MUST instrument before adding more code, to discriminate (a) vs (b) vs (c).\n\n\n**Goal:** discriminate seed-ordering vs qualified_name-None for the residual user→user CallDirect targets, then apply the targeted fix.\n\n1. **Reapply Track B's source patches** (the user_function_name_map infrastructure + seed loop + fixup walk + cranelift call-site fallback). DO NOT skip this step — instrumentation needs the map to exist.\n\n2. **Add a pre-fixup dump** at the top of `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` in `compiler/src/compilation.rs`:\n   - `eprintln!(\"[user_fn_map] size={}\", user_function_name_map.len());`\n   - For each entry: `eprintln!(\"[user_fn_map] {} → {:?}\", qname, irfn_id);`\n   - Also emit the same for every USER MIR module's `func.qualified_name` + `func.name` + `func.id` to a parallel `[user_fn_table]` channel.\n\n3. **Run llama-chat cold once.** Search the stderr for `280007` and `220015`:\n   - **If both ids appear as VALUES in `[user_fn_map]` lines** → seed ordering is the bug. The map is populated correctly; the fixup just runs against the caller's module before the callee's seed entry exists for THAT WALK. Fix by hoisting the seed to BEFORE the fixup pass invocation in `compile_ast_with_shared_state` control flow (post-merge, pre-fixup, ONCE for all user modules at once).\n   - **If neither id appears as a VALUE in `[user_fn_map]` BUT does appear in `[user_fn_table]`** → seed code is too restrictive (probably the `if func.cfg.blocks.is_empty() { continue; }` guard is skipping the callee). Drop the empty-CFG guard and rely on the existing Sweep 2 oracle (cached_stdlib_mir \u003e import_mir_modules \u003e mir_modules) to find the real body.\n   - **If neither id appears in EITHER channel** → the IrFunctionIds aren't routable by qualified_name at all (monomorph specialisations with `qualified_name=None`). Fix by extending the seed to also write under bare `func.name` with a prefix marker (e.g. `__bare__:` + name) AND extending the cranelift_backend.rs fallback to attempt bare-name lookup when `qualified_name.as_deref()` returns None. OR (better long-term): fix monomorphization to propagate the parent generic's qualified_name onto its specialisations.\n\n4. **After landing the targeted fix, re-run** the 10-cold canonical measurement. Expected: exit_histogram `{\"0\":10}` (or `{\"0\":9, \"133\":1}` if the SIGTRAP 133 residual is still live — that's a separate bug class).\n\n5. **Two-sweep validation:** apply the same two-sweep pattern the stdlib map already has (Sweep 1 retain-live; Sweep 2 rewrite stub-target → real-body oracle) to the user map. Currently absent in Track B's design.\n\n\n\n**Surface:** W0020 warnings on cold-clean compile:\n- `cannot compile nue.arch.BertArch.build; reason: Called function IrFunctionId(280007) not found in module`\n- Same for `nue.arch.LlamaArch.build`\n- `cannot compile nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build; reason: IrFunctionId(220015) not found in module`\n\n**Class:** stale-CallDirect trap-stub cascade, but in USER-MODULE territory (b013900 only addresses stdlib).\n\n**Fix path:** Next-iteration plan above. Track B's infrastructure + pre-fixup instrumentation discriminates the three sub-mechanisms.\n\n**Observable impact:** Currently silent (functions trap-stub; callers either don't invoke them on the canonical paris path or invoke them and get the 1/10 exit 133). The cold-clean 9/10 success rate exists DESPITE these residuals, because the canonical paris path doesn't depend on the build entry points that trap-stub.\n\n\n**Surface:** ~1/10 cold-clean runs exit 133 (SIGTRAP) AFTER `[gen] streaming...` prints but BEFORE the first token emits. Content-dependent: short prompts fail more often than the canonical paris prompt; on the canonical prompt the rate has been measured at 1-2/10 post-b013900.\n\n**Class:** NOT the trap-stub cascade. Path E + cranelift_backend assert would emit a loud E0600 if a CallDirect were unresolved at JIT time, and they don't. The crash is in or near KVCache.append at seq_pos=1 (first decode call, KV cache holding only the prompt tokens), with sub-mechanisms ranked in `bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption.md` H-kvcache-edge:\n1. KVCache.append at seq_pos=prompt_len with content-specific shape mismatch.\n2. Attention mask construction at seq_pos=1 producing OOB index for specific KV depths.\n3. GQA head-expansion stride correct for prefill `[T, hidden]` but wrong for decode `[1, hidden]` at certain promptLen values.\n4. Value-domain NaN/inf from RoPE pair-split or expandKvHeads emitting denormals — `bmmThreaded(kT, 0)` could trap on FP signal.\n\nH-kvcache-append-stride was refuted in this session (contiguous-workaround discriminator showed no rate change). Remaining sub-mechanisms 2-4 are open.\n\n**Diagnostic next step:** eprintln K/V shape + strides + finite-count + |max|/|mean| inside `GQAttention.forward` immediately before `cache.append(k, v)`; also dump qByHead/kT/vAllExpanded shape+strides before `bmmThreaded`. Compare success vs fail across 10 cold short-prompt runs.\n\n\n```bash\nfind . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +\ncargo clean\n\nRAYZOR_TIER=cranelift cargo run --release -- run nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx \\\n  \"What is the capital of France?\"\n\nRAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1 cargo run --release -- run nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx \"test\"\n\n```\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_session_2026_06_compiler_arc` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}